<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:windows">
<!--
    kuna stackexitanchor: the epilogue settles an unknown callee's purge.

    A hand-written __stdcall caller (x86win, so every call's extrapop is
    unknown and StackSolver runs):
        f:  push 2 ; push 1 ; mov eax,[esp+0xc] ; call eax      ; callee pops 8
            mov ecx,[esp+4] ; mov eax,[esp+8] ; add eax,ecx ; ret 8
    The callee is reached through a pointer, so nothing states its cleanup and
    the push-run guess credits it with one argument.  Every stack reference
    after the call is then 4 bytes off: the second argument reads the return
    address slot.  The return instruction pops the caller's return address, so
    the stack pointer entering it equals the entry one; with the option on that
    is an exact equation, and the call's purge is solved backward from it.

    Stage: P6 stack-pointer flow, option stackexitanchor off|on.  Pass 1 (off)
    pins the bug, pass 2 (on, the default) pins the fix.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401000">
6a026a018b44240cffd08b4c24048b44240801c8c20800
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401000" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option stackexitanchor off</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option stackexitanchor on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="STACKEXITANCHOR #1: off, the call is credited with one argument" min="1" max="1">^  \(\*a0\)\(1\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="STACKEXITANCHOR #2: off, the sum reads the return-address slot" min="1" max="1">int4 v1; // eax \| stack \+ 0x0</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="STACKEXITANCHOR #3: on, the call takes both pushed arguments" min="1" max="1">^  \(\*a0\)\(1,2\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="STACKEXITANCHOR #4: on, f returns its two arguments' sum" min="1" max="1">^  return &amp;a0\[a1\];$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
